# The Great Defeats Achariyar Suffered
Tamil title: ஆச்சாரியார் அடைந்த மாபெரும் தோல்விகள்

Four months have now passed since the Congress ministry was formed. Under the rule of the Honourable Rajagopalachari in Madras, the clown danced, crying, “Have we not beheld things never seen before?” Joy was everywhere and blessings accumulated. Brahmin hearts swelled as they chanted, “Our Raj has arrived!” Opponents of imperialism sharpened their swords, spears and lances and roared heroically, “We are going to tear the new reforms to pieces!” Socialists spoke Marxism: “We shall tax the rich more heavily, bring comfort to the poor and drive out the zamindars!” Workers thundered, “Capitalists can no longer show us their true form. This is our Raj!” Truly, no ministry before this one had ever been formed amid so many blessings and welcomes. Its beginning was certainly auspicious.

## Formidable Strength

The Madras ministry possesses formidable strength. Its majority is large enough to intimidate its opponents. The head of the ministry is a man of extraordinary ability, connected by marriage to the Mahatma—the Kulluka Bhattar renowned for intrigue. The power of the press is pledged to labour at washing and drinking the water from the lotus feet of the ministry, seeking deliverance from sin and fulfilment in this birth.

The leaders of the opposition are marvels unseen anywhere else in politics. One is a former governor; another is a prince. For some reason both have become absorbed in the ministerial mansion and locked their mouths. What affection they show the Premier! Whatever he does, what admiration! They praise him lavishly, shake his hand, offer garlands and banquets, and swell with pride at the “blessing” of saying *tathastu* to Achariyar.

As though these two “great leaders” and all these forts and battlements were insufficient, there is also the slavery called party discipline, preventing differences of opinion or bitterness from arising—or, if they arise, preventing members from breathing a word of them outside. For this, members receive another seventy-five rupees a month from public funds.

Is that all? The provincial head, His Excellency the Governor, sits in the Premier's ticket pocket. Though the Viceroy gave no verbal assurance, the Working Committee has declared that he gave one in his heart. What influence! A ministry of this kind could carry out any epoch-making act it wished. If it possessed the will and knew the way, it could enact countless reforms. It has that much authority.

## Then and Now

The previous ministry possessed very little strength. That was the age of dyarchy. British officials and the Governor held the vital arteries of authority in their own hands. Those ministers did not control important departments such as the treasury and police. Furthermore, every newspaper opposed that ministry. Congressmen continually attacked those ministers, and the entire Brahmin community of the Madras Presidency cursed it. How many petty issues! How many intrigues! How much conspiracy took place within the legislature!

This same Honourable Rajagopalachari incited Dr Subbarayan against the Bobbili ministry and created rebellion within the Justice Party itself. That ministry coped with all these pressures.

Today's ministry, by contrast, is “the darling child of the people.” It has no political opposition. How great, then, ought its prowess to be? What exalted deeds should it have accomplished? How many victories should it have won by now? If we examine the matter, we find ourselves obliged to offer the ministry our sympathy.

Alas, the balance sheet of Kulluka Bhattar and Company—stock on hand, total receipts, expenditure and net profit—has ended at zero. The company's bankruptcy and voluntary liquidation are not far away.

## Have They Torn It? Have They Even Shaken It?

Why was the Congress ministry formed? What did they say during the election? Did they not say they would tear up the new reforms? Did they not say they would shatter imperialism? Have they torn even an atom of it in the course of that work? Have they even shaken it?

Congress propagandists shouted themselves hoarse that they would break the new reforms. This Premier said, “Just as Hitler, upon coming to power, tore up the enslaving Treaty of Versailles, we shall act.” What condition are the new reforms in today? Is any attempt being made to tear them up? What is the method—surrendering to the Governor, or dining and rejoicing with him?

Those with courage equal to the ministry's task of destroying the new reforms would honourably admit this failure. Pure heroes who cared nothing for self-interest and kept their word would not tolerate such a defeat. Where is that “courage”?

## Where Is the Courage?

“We shall not be slaves of British rule or servants of imperialism. We shall seize the British lion by its beard and shake it. We shall make the Governor tremble. We shall cut the salaries of the ICS class and subdue their arrogance.” Does the ministry that once spoke with such fiery courage possess it today, as it shakes hands with the Governor, bares its teeth and fondles him?

What does its shameless declaration that the Governor is its friend, guide and spiritual teacher reveal, if not a servile mentality? Could a genuine lover of freedom behold this defeat without shedding tears of blood?

## Surrender to the “Traitor”

The Premier once condemned Dr Rajan for betraying Congress; because of it, Rajan donned ochre, took up the ascetic's water pot and departed for the forest. But what a pitiable condition now! The Premier had to surrender to that same “traitor,” Dr Rajan, and include him in his ministry. Can a political leader suffer a more humiliating defeat? The weight of Dr Rajan produced a defeat capable of making “Rajaji's” nerves tremble, yet he endured it. Why? It was not the first defeat of his political life, though still greater defeats awaited him.

## The “Flag” Fight

As soon as the ministry was formed, did the Premier's attempt to have Congress's tricolour flown over local bodies as the national flag succeed? The Governor declared that it could not be accepted as the national flag. The minister saluted, saying, “Very well, my lord,” and the government order avoided the term *national flag*, calling it merely the Congress party flag.

Would not defeat in this brief fight have made the bodies of manly heroes wither? The quarrel gave the Premier an occasion to show the Governor his courage and the greatness of Congress. What did he display? He bared his teeth and suffered defeat. Even Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan, celebrated as the Frontier Gandhi, declared that the Congress flag was not the national flag.

## Indianisation

They boasted that under their rule the entire official establishment would be Indianised. For a month or two Achariyar appointed Dr A. Lakshmanaswami Mudaliar principal of Madras Medical College. But when the British official class and the Governor began exerting pressure, he returned the post to a European. In Indianisation too, he found only defeat.

## Were the Debts Cancelled?

Some ten or twelve years ago, our supremely expert Premier moved and carried a resolution that the national debts should be cancelled and would not be repaid. Once the Congress Raj arrived, however, raising a new loan required him to sign for the old debt as well. Unable to balance expenditure within revenue, he borrowed another one and a half crores of rupees to balance the budget.

We were told they could govern splendidly without debt. Was it a great victory that their life in office began with a begging bowl and created a debtor government? Managing the position by borrowing one and a half crores was another of Achariyar's great defeats.

## Did Taxes Fall?

“Taxes are going to fall! Taxes are going to fall!” Congress devotees cried joyfully. Even those called leaders boasted that they could devise a more generous remission of land revenue than the plans of the Justice ministers and the interim ministers. Did they manage it? Could they reduce even a single copper beyond the interim ministry's scheme of seventy-five lakhs?

They boasted that Achariyar's brain was magnificent and that, somehow, he would reduce taxation. “Just watch,” they said. What did he accomplish? What occurred? Intrigues are now under way to impose new taxes. Defeat here too—and a crushing defeat.

## Cutting the Collector's Salary

Achariyar's men once spoke ferociously: “Only because these Justice men and other reactionaries are ministers do they pour out plunderous salaries to the British ICS class. Would that happen if we came to power? Would we waste the tax money of poor India in this fashion? Would we not slash the European's salary?”

Did they accomplish it? Achariyar now plays fondly with British officials. How beautifully he elevates them, saying they are like delicate plants that must be protected in the shade! Without the least shame, he attempted to cut their salaries and obtained precisely nothing—defeat, defeat and defeat again.

The Premier travelled about daily, baring his teeth and begging officials, of their own generosity and from an understanding that India was poor, voluntarily to surrender part of their salaries. What happened? How many officials reduced their pay? What name can be given to this disgrace? Achariyar was defeated in this attempt too.

## Where Is the Economy?

They said Congress ministers would not increase expenditure by drawing salaries of five thousand rupees like others; their salary would be only five hundred. Their frugal life and modest salary, it was said, would benefit the poor and simple.

How much lower, then, was this ministry's net expenditure than that of the preceding ministry? Listen to the measure of its economy. I cite the Premier's own budget speech. The reduced expenditure supposedly saved twenty thousand rupees. Of that, eighteen thousand was spent on buying six motor cars. After Achariyar finished speaking, the remaining two thousand was spent as well: a motor car was bought for the legislative Speaker, the shirtless Honourable S. Satyamurti.

You may now calculate the result yourselves. The economy programme yielded zero. Alas, he met proper defeat here too.

## A Blow to the Moratorium's Head

What fate befell the “moratorium” introduced to earn the title “Protector of the Cultivators”? Lawyers raged, and Achariyar immediately withdrew. When the Governor intervened, he threw the moratorium into the waste-paper basket. His intention failed here as well. Poor man—defeated!

## All Came to Nothing

Achariyar's class had also drawn up many “plans”: separation of the judiciary from the executive, reduction of court fees and division of provinces on a linguistic basis. Once on a public platform, they would speak of these until the very firmament split, saying the country's deliverance depended upon them. They sought votes by saying the ruined Justice Party must go so that these “wish-fulfilling cows” might become ours.

What happened after they occupied the ministerial throne? Could court fees be reduced? No. In which province was the judiciary separated from the executive? None. It will not happen. In none of these three matters could Achariyar achieve anything in practice. He met defeat.

## Has Repression Disappeared?

“When Bobbili rule goes and our rule arrives, evils such as Section 144, Section 124A and the CID will disappear. Freedom of speech and writing will spread its fragrance,” declared these heroes of liberty. They have now been crowned. What happened? Did repression disappear? No.

They themselves imposed prohibitory orders under Section 144. Under Section 124A they punished even one of their own comrades. The CID continues to follow the extremists among them. Newspaper securities, house searches and baton charges—Achariyar could abolish none of them. He now says these instruments are necessary. See how speech changes upon ascending the throne.

Could Achariyar move even one letter of the machinery of repression? Did he achieve victory in abolishing it? Alas, poor man! He met only defeat.

Whenever strikes occurred in the past, Achariyar's class said they arose from the cruelty of having no democratic government. They said that if they, who loved labour, came to power, workers would suffer no trouble and peaceful conciliation would prevail.

Their government arrived, but where is conciliation? It is needed, yet Achariyar could not provide it. Strikes have occurred in many places since the ministry was formed, and workers' distress has not been wiped away. The Labour Minister's stratagem failed. Comrades are enraged, and a hunger strike is taking place in Coimbatore. Did Achariyar succeed there in governing so that labour disputes did not arise? Defeat there too.

## Communalism—A Forest Fire

“Communalism is like a forest fire; it scorches nationalism. That fire will be extinguished in the flood of the election,” they said. Their inner meaning was that the Justice Party had produced communal representation and that they would abolish it and Brahminise the official establishment. That was their election cry. Every Brahmin in the South held his breath and laboured for it with wife and children.

Could Achariyar move that cherished policy? The Governor stands ready to intervene, and non-Brahmin feeling exists. Realising that his former boast could not succeed, Achariyar admitted defeat and promised in the legislature to act according to the policy of communal representation.

## The Complete-Prohibition Hoax

The ministers have declared that the miracle demonstrated in Salem district cannot be performed in the other districts; that the budget does not permit prohibition across the whole Presidency; and that extending it to other districts would leave no grants for education, roads or medicine. Complete prohibition has therefore been abandoned. A great defeat there too.

Prohibition continues in Salem only as an ornament of speech for propaganda. Even there, drinkers simply pass their days by taking a little more trouble, being more alert or spending more money to obtain liquor. When this is its character in a single district, they have evidently wondered who could wrestle with it throughout the Presidency and abandoned the plan for complete prohibition. The ministers have indirectly admitted their defeat.

## Collision with Untouchability

A conference of Brahmin scriptural scholars declared that untouchability would disappear only through death. Achariyar's class does not say so. Since Achariyar, who praised the Maharaja of Travancore, was in power, everyone expected an Untouchability Abolition Bill. He and his colleagues had spoken in that tone.

But that attempt too has failed. They have begun offering the excuse that legislation cannot be enacted hastily and that public opinion is required. What does this reveal? Defeat alone.

## The Hindi Battle

Achariyar is suffering defeat over compulsory Hindi as well; one might say he has already been defeated. He expected Tamil Nadu to say *tathastu* the moment he spoke. Otherwise, before the ministry was even formed, would he have promised a secret gathering of certain northern leaders that he would make Hindi compulsory if he became a minister?

Tamils began condemning Achariyar's course. They girded themselves and set out, resolved to test their strength against him. What could the Premier do? He told stories; they did not listen. He became angry and tried abusing them as ignoramuses; the Tamils did not submit. He now lies subdued like a snake in a box.

The Education Minister bargains that although Hindi will be compulsory, there will be no examination in it. Can a subject without an examination be called compulsory? Splendid, splendid! Their only thought now is that northern leaders will mock them when they see the defeat suffered over Hindi. Thus defeat is certain here too.

## Bobbili's Scissors

They said the Bobbili ministry had cut up and ruined the district boards, and that nomination had been used to preserve party strength. “As soon as we come to power, we shall reunite the district boards cut apart by Bobbili's scissors.” Achariyar was defeated in that attempt too.

The moment the Trichinopoly board was reunited, Congress found itself unable to install a Congress president despite possessing a majority. Achariyar was deeply disappointed. This “sample” was enough to frighten the ministry; there will be no more programme of reunification.

Here is an amusing example. A delegation went to the Honourable Gopala Reddy asking for the Ramanathapuram district boards to be reunited, and conferences passed resolutions. Yet they were not reunited. The Honourable Gopala Reddy toured Ramanathapuram. The president of the board for one part of North Ramanathapuram district, V. T. T. Venkatachalam Chettiar, was a Justice man and the son-in-law of Raja Sir Annamalai Chettiar. The minister was apparently welcomed and given a delightful banquet. Need one describe the magnificence of a rich man's feast?

I do not say the banquet bewitched the minister into abandoning reunification. If V. T. T. is a Chettinad magnate, the Honourable Gopala Reddy is a young Kubera of Andhra. What, then, is the mystery behind the board not being reunited? There is a reason. Who is Leader of the Opposition in the legislature? The Justice “hero,” Kumararaja Muthiah.

All the rites are proceeding so that the old performance may occur again. Today or tomorrow the bargain will be completed, and those ministers will once more sign the bond of servitude. Meanwhile, people must not forget the principal features of the drama that has occurred.

To use *Dinamani*'s language: “Panchali stands in the royal assembly. Her garment has been stripped away and her honour flies in the wind. Her husbands writhe, yet Dharmar asks them to remain patient.” That is what caught *Dinamani*'s attention. How pitiable!

In that age, Panchali's husbands “remained patient” while her honour was attacked. The episode in which self-respect was lost, once explained through mythology, is now being enacted upon a “national” foundation as another disrobing of Panchali. But will a fight occur? No.

What will happen instead? These mighty heroes say: “Members of the assembly! They have removed Panchali's sari. This is a most cruel deed and anger truly boils within us. Look at our muscles—we are no ordinary men. Yes, we know how to fight. But there must be no fight now. Please close your eyes for one minute so Panchali may put her loosened sari back on. She is a woman, after all; even a demon pities a woman.”

The United Provinces and Bihar ministers ordered the release of some fifty prisoners. Their order received no respect. The Viceroy rendered it worthless and used his authority. The ministers resigned.

## Where Is the Courage?

That, in brief, is what occurred. What must happen for a remedy to be obtained?

The Viceroy must admit that his intervention in the ministers' matter was wrong, express regret or apology, and promise never to act in that manner again. That is the proper course if the affair is to proceed with self-respect.

The issue will not be resolved if those prisoners are released merely because their terms have expired or after their individual cases are reviewed. Why? Because that is a normal occurrence requiring none of this thunderous drama. When a sentence expires, the prison gate opens by itself. Releasing prisoners after examining their individual eligibility happens routinely; even non-political prisoners have been released that way. Hundreds of political prisoners convicted of violence were released in Bengal by this method.

If that alone is now to happen in Bihar and the United Provinces, where is the heroism? If the ministers return to office before the Viceroy apologises for the act that caused their resignations, it will be a deed without honour.

If those ministers decide that this is nevertheless what they must do, let them please come immediately to Courtallam and meet our Premier there. He will say: “Madmen! You made such a commotion over prisoners. Is releasing prisoners a reason to fight? In my Presidency I have arrested Batliwala and Krishna Pillai and kept them inside!” Then they may all enjoy the Courtallam waterfall together and compose poetry.

*Kudi Arasu*, 26 December 1937
